From 7c967e3f636ef240b155e1b6dd7bbfc020d2d35c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slopdealer <326519127+slopdealer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:33:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 43/49] Red team fix 1: check's streaming decode keeps
 upstream's ceiling on decoded bytes

check --read-data hashes the decoded bytes of a compressed blob as a stream.
A stream limits only the window, so a crafted blob that decodes to more than
64 GiB was accepted, while upstream's check (which decodes into memory with
zstd's default limit) and restore refuse it.

The stream's output is now counted against the same limit, 64 GiB, and
output beyond it fails with the decoder's own error for that case, in the
wrapping the eager path uses, so the message is upstream's. A first frame
that declares more content than the limit is refused before any of it is
decoded, as DecodeAll does. Blobs too short to reach the limit (less than
limit>>21 compressed bytes, 32 KiB for 64 GiB) take the path they took
before, without the counter.

Tests (seat 2 finding 1, carried in): TestCheckStreamKeepsCumulativeDecodeLimit
fails before this commit and passes after. The reviewer set the small
ceiling of the quick test on the decoder, which a stream cannot read; the
test sets the same ceiling on the iterator as well (one line), and now also
compares the error text with the eager path and the exact boundary.
TestCheckStreamRefusesDeclaredOversize covers the declared size.
TestLargeCheckRejectsUnrestorableBlob is the production-size test (64 GiB
+ 1 byte, opt-in with RESTIC_LARGE_CHECK_TEST=1), unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
